### FIX 1: CORRECT STRETCHING MATH

Audit fit_to_duration().

Ensure:

ratio = target_duration / raw_duration
tempo = clamp(ratio, 0.9, 1.1)

NOT inverted.

After atempo:
- Re-measure duration
- Apply trim/pad to reach target ±20ms

--------------------------------------------------
### FIX 2: TWO-PASS PER-SEGMENT NORMALIZATION

For each segment:

PASS 1:
- TTS → raw.wav
- Stretch → stretched.wav

PASS 2:
- Measure stretched.wav
- If |error| > 0.02s:
    - If too long → trim
    - If too short → pad silence
- Produce final seg_X.wav

No segment may exceed target by >20ms.

--------------------------------------------------
### FIX 3: YOUTUBE-STYLE CUMULATIVE CORRECTION

Implement rolling sync correction.

Maintain:

cumulative_error = 0.0

For each segment i:

effective_target = (end-start) - cumulative_error

Fit segment to effective_target.

After fitting:

segment_error = final - effective_target
cumulative_error += segment_error

Clamp cumulative_error to ±0.3s.

This prevents drift buildup.

This is how large platforms stabilize long videos.

--------------------------------------------------
### FIX 4: GLOBAL NORMALIZATION (FINAL SAFETY NET)

After concatenation:

audio_len = get_audio_duration(dubbed.wav)
video_len = get_video_duration(input.mp4)

If |audio_len - video_len| > 0.3s:

- If audio > video → trim to video_len
- If audio < video → pad to video_len

Save as:

dubbed_fixed.wav

Use ONLY dubbed_fixed.wav for muxing.

--------------------------------------------------
### FIX 5: REMOVE TRIMMING DURING MUX

Audit ffmpeg commands.

REMOVE:
-shortest

Final mux must be:

ffmpeg -y \
  -i input.mp4 \
  -i dubbed_fixed.wav \
  -map 0:v:0 \
  -map 1:a:0 \
  -c:v copy \
  -c:a aac \
  -movflags +faststart \
  out.mp4

Never rely on muxer to fix timing.

